#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186356748 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b428331b-4916-5b2c-8aa6-89a2358cdef0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Indefinite Integral of a Exponential-Trigonometric Expression Using Integration by Parts"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c64b762-3594-5230-9187-4ade49f77b04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-3x}}{10}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356750} {:answer/id #uuid "a339d273-c2d3-55cd-9e5d-84c14b00b488" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-3x}}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356751} {:answer/id #uuid "4c8ae58a-afc5-50f8-a878-1e3f872fd601" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-3x}}{5}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356752} {:answer/id #uuid "739a3986-b969-5b56-9baa-0e61c62513f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-3x}}{10}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356753} {:answer/id #uuid "ef7646b2-fc02-5cd6-99b3-412708d6252a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-3x}}{4}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356754}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "739a3986-b969-5b56-9baa-0e61c62513f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-3x}}{10}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356753} :answer-field/id #uuid "042a3d3b-21c3-580b-b9e3-0da837e2365a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356749}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4c99d56a-1377-5ce5-b516-30c385b48d3f" :question/math-academy-id "q-114950" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int {e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "To use integration by parts, let's suppose that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-3x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-3{e}^{-3x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int {e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-3x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-\\int (\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(-3{e}^{-3x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3\\int {e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we can use integration by parts to evaluate\n\n$$\nJ=\\int {e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-3x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-3{e}^{-3x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J & =\\int {e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-3x}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(-3{e}^{-3x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-3\\int {e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-3I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this back into our equation for $I,$ we can subsequently solve for $I.$ We remember to add a constant of integration $C$ at the end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3J \\\\ I & ={e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3(-{e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-3I) \\\\ I & ={e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3{e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-9I \\\\ 10I & ={e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3{e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ I & =\\frac{{e}^{-3x}}{10}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186356755 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b428331b-4916-5b2c-8aa6-89a2358cdef0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Indefinite Integral of a Exponential-Trigonometric Expression Using Integration by Parts"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "185bbe98-9f84-509f-bc39-572b0ee53fc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)-{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)-{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)}{34}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356757} {:answer/id #uuid "fc8c142d-dc77-5fc5-82fb-3a1a763a776b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)-3{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)-3{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)}{34}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356758} {:answer/id #uuid "180b66e7-b823-5b42-ba6f-13d0fba0111a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{3x}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x))}{34}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356759} {:answer/id #uuid "078965f0-0441-558a-8372-3920df2cacb4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{3x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x))}{34}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356760} {:answer/id #uuid "14c600c0-fd9c-5ecf-9ef1-eb3cb319ba8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)+{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)-{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)}{34}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356761}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "180b66e7-b823-5b42-ba6f-13d0fba0111a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{3x}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x))}{34}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356759} :answer-field/id #uuid "e284cfe5-d554-5487-a349-610fd8979757" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356756}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "da447264-0928-56ee-9624-ed9ccec3c043" :question/math-academy-id "q-12921" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int {e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "To use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{3x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3{e}^{3x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int {e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)}{5})-\\int (-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)}{5})(3{e}^{3x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)}{5}+\\frac{3}{5}\\int {e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)}{5}+\\frac{3}{5}J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we can use integration by parts to evaluate\n\n$$\nJ=\\int {e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{3x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3{e}^{3x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J & =\\int {e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)}{5})-\\int (\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)}{5})(3{e}^{3x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)}{5}-\\frac{3}{5}\\int {e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)}{5}-\\frac{3}{5}I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this back into our equation for $I,$ we can subsequently solve for $I.$ We remember to add a constant of integration $C$ at the end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =-\\frac{{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)}{5}+\\frac{3}{5}J \\\\ I & =-\\frac{{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)}{5}+\\frac{3}{5}(\\frac{{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)}{5}-\\frac{3}{5}I) \\\\ I & =-\\frac{{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)}{5}+\\frac{3{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)}{25}-\\frac{9}{25}I \\\\ \\frac{34}{25}I & =\\frac{3{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)-5{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)}{25} \\\\ I & =\\frac{{e}^{3x}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x))}{34}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186356762 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b428331b-4916-5b2c-8aa6-89a2358cdef0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Indefinite Integral of a Exponential-Trigonometric Expression Using Integration by Parts"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ef328b71-5429-5c24-b16d-c38538c954b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{5}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356764} {:answer/id #uuid "2ee232f3-25a5-5b03-815a-c7b8ae0868ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-x}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))}{10}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356765} {:answer/id #uuid "5c356490-5840-5014-801a-171475c60f35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-x}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))}{5}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356766} {:answer/id #uuid "539c164c-7a9b-5536-876e-f666e199a2e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))}{10}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356767} {:answer/id #uuid "55a09822-55c1-51d9-bbb2-2a2c416bcde6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-x}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))}{5}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356768}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "55a09822-55c1-51d9-bbb2-2a2c416bcde6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-x}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))}{5}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356768} :answer-field/id #uuid "2cb3061d-e7e3-519e-81c2-85723c01683e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356763}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4e567139-f9e4-517d-849c-624876b64e25" :question/math-academy-id "q-3398" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "To use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-{e}^{-x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2})-\\int (\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2})(-{e}^{-x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we can use integration by parts to evaluate\n\n$$\nJ=\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-{e}^{-x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J & =\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2})-\\int (-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2})(-{e}^{-x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this back into our equation for $I,$ we can subsequently solve for $I.$ We remember to add a constant of integration $C$ at the end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}J \\\\ I & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}I) \\\\ I & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{4}-\\frac{1}{4}I \\\\ \\frac{5}{4}I & =\\frac{2{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)-{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{4} \\\\ I & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))}{5}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186356769 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1da25ef-5d1b-588f-b1cc-121562ff98ea" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Definite Integral of a Exponential-Trigonometric Expression Using Integration by Parts"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f1b29cec-586c-53e9-a42e-acae9c1e9747" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+e^{-\\pi/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356771}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f1b29cec-586c-53e9-a42e-acae9c1e9747" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+e^{-\\pi/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356771} :answer-field/id #uuid "47ed4d29-79e3-5321-9125-4aaaf4cd2e1f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356770}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b311e86d-b621-57b8-a97e-35353c4497de" :question/math-academy-id "q-362667" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{2}{5}($\n{{field-1}} $)$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the indefinite integral by  $I=\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-{e}^{-x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({e}^{-x})\\,(-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))-\\int (-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))(-{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-\\frac{1}{2}J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we can use integration by parts to evaluate\n\n$$\nJ=\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-{e}^{-x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J & =\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({e}^{-x})\\,(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))-\\int (\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))(-{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+\\frac{1}{2}I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation for $I,$ we solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+\\frac{1}{2}I) \\\\ I & =-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)-\\frac{1}{4}I \\\\ \\frac{5}{4}I & =-{e}^{-x}(\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)) \\\\ I & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{5}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & =(-\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{5}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))){|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =(-\\frac{{e}^{-\\pi /2}}{5}(0-2))-(-\\frac{1}{5}(0+2)) \\\\  & =\\frac{2{e}^{-\\pi /2}}{5}+\\frac{2}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}(1+{e}^{-\\pi /2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186356772 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1da25ef-5d1b-588f-b1cc-121562ff98ea" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Definite Integral of a Exponential-Trigonometric Expression Using Integration by Parts"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e341b494-3998-5018-b341-2b525d4a54d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}({e}^{-\\pi /3}+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356774} {:answer/id #uuid "217c10ff-b118-5cdf-a876-f839638f7775" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{-\\pi /3}-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356775} {:answer/id #uuid "6ffccd6f-179c-535e-bc4e-0f61c4ad7278" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{-\\pi /3}+10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356776} {:answer/id #uuid "0a6f3e76-2de7-5303-ae1b-616ef0f116d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{10}({e}^{-\\pi /3}+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356777} {:answer/id #uuid "44858f29-3437-5bb0-a520-df8a3e6780fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}({e}^{-\\pi /3}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356778}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0a6f3e76-2de7-5303-ae1b-616ef0f116d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{10}({e}^{-\\pi /3}+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356777} :answer-field/id #uuid "6da96dba-f1f8-51f2-ab01-312cf0472440" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356773}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6014a799-6357-511b-912c-689499b2d877" :question/math-academy-id "q-83014" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the indefinite integral by $I=\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-{e}^{-x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3})-\\int (-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3})(-{e}^{-x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we can use integration by parts to evaluate\n\n$$\nJ=\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-{e}^{-x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J & =\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3})-\\int (\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3})(-{e}^{-x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting this into our equation for $I,$ we can subsequently solve for $I:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}J \\\\ I & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}I) \\\\ I & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{9}-\\frac{1}{9}I \\\\ \\frac{10}{9}I & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{9} \\\\ I & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate our definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =(-\\frac{{e}^{-x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))}{10}){|}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-\\pi /3}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3})+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}))}{10}+\\frac{{e}^{-0}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot 0)+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot 0))}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{e}^{-\\pi /3}}{10}+\\frac{3}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}({e}^{-\\pi /3}+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
